# rocm-exporter.service — **unavailable on this host** (do not install)

## Status: not installable

The `amd/rocm-exporter` route to GPU metrics is **unavailable** and this unit is
**not** part of the deployed stack. The previous version of this document (and
of [monitoring/README.md](../../monitoring/README.md)) instructed installing it
from a GitHub release tarball; that release does not exist.

### Why it is unavailable

- **No published artefacts.** The `amd/rocm-exporter` repository publishes no
  releases, so the download URLs the earlier docs used
  (`…/releases/download/v<version>/rocm-exporter-<version>.linux-amd64.tar.gz`)
  return 404. Verified 2026-10-25 by querying the GitHub releases API for the
  repository (empty list) — there is nothing to download, so the binary can
  never be installed from a release.
- **Nothing listens on port 5000.** Because no exporter ever ran, the
  Prometheus scrape job that targeted `localhost:5000` was permanently `DOWN`.
  That job is now commented out in
  [monitoring/prometheus.yml](../../monitoring/prometheus.yml) with the same
  explanation.
- **The series it would have published never existed.** Rules selecting
  `rocm_vram_used_bytes` / `rocm_vram_total_bytes` could never be satisfied, so
  they were removed (`monitoring/gpu_vram_alerts.yaml`); GPU memory pressure is
  now measured on GTT — see
  [monitoring/gpu_memory_alerts.yaml](../../monitoring/gpu_memory_alerts.yaml).

Do not re-enable the `rocm-exporter` scrape job or re-add `rocm_vram_*` rules
without first confirming that a working exporter exists and is running: an
unbacked target is permanently `DOWN`, and a rule over a series nothing
publishes can never fire.

## What is used instead

GPU metrics come from a route that needs no unpublished binary:

| Need | Provided by | Series |
|---|---|---|
| GPU utilisation | node_exporter `--collector.hwmon` (GPU package power) + `gpu_compute_rules.yaml` | `node_hwmon_power_average_watt{chip_name="amdgpu"}` → `gpu:power_watts:amdgpu`, `gpu:computing:bool` |
| GPU memory (the pool models actually use) | 10 s sysfs sampler → node_exporter `--collector.textfile.directory` | `gpu_gtt_used_bytes` |
| GPU memory (BIOS carve-out, informational) | same sampler | `gpu_vram_used_bytes`, `gpu_vram_total_bytes` |

- [docs/systemd/node_exporter.service](node_exporter.service) — the exporter
  that serves both the hwmon and textfile series.
- [docs/systemd/gpu-metrics.service](gpu-metrics.service) — the 10 s sysfs
  sampler and its metric names.
- [monitoring/gpu_compute_rules.yaml](../../monitoring/gpu_compute_rules.yaml) —
  why utilisation is derived from power rather than `gpu_busy_percent`.

## If AMD ever publishes releases again

The shape this unit would take is unchanged and is worth recording, so the
decision can be revisited cheaply:

- binary installed under `~/bin/rocm-exporter/`;
- `--web.listen-address=127.0.0.1:5000` — **loopback only**, for the same reason
  the other exporters are loopback only: they have no authentication and are
  scraped from the same host;
- a `rocm-exporter` scrape job in `monitoring/prometheus.yml` targeting
  `localhost:5000`, plus rules that are then satisfied by real series.

Before adopting it, verify the artefacts and the series they publish, and
confirm the added value over the sampler (on this APU the carve-out VRAM figure
is the least useful memory number, because models live in GTT).

